;;
	a static memory layout is declared at the top level as:
	sym : [ members ]

	the syntax inside the brackets is the same as for
	dynamic memory layouts

	static layouts live in the read-only data section of
	the program, they exist for the whole run of the
	program, and there is exactly one instance of each

	the name evaluates to the base address of the layout
	as a pointer, so pointer arithmetic on it indexes
	into the layout by byte (for now)

	the type of the pointer is inferred from the first
	primitive in the layout, so `sym+` advances by that
	type's size when used with the type-aware operators

	the size-specific operators write to and read from
	a memory address:
	@1 @2 @4 @8 write a u1, u2, u4 or u8 to the address
	!1 !2 !4 !8 read a u1, u2, u4 or u8 from the address

	@ and ! without a size infer the size from the top
	value's type when it is a primitive

	write operators consume the address and a to write value
	read operators consume the address and push the read value
;;


staticmem:[256 s4]

main:(){
    0 while *> 256 <{ ; n
        *> *> ; n n n
        staticmem+ ; n n  (staticmem n +) 
        @1; n (n  (staticmem n +) @1)
        1 +; (n 1 +)
    } //

    0 while *> 256 <{ ; n
        *> staticmem + ; n (staticmem n +) 
        !1; n ((staticmem n +) !1)
        dmp; n
        1 +; (n 1 +)
    } //
}